 "notes": {
  "sheet": "A gauge number is not one thickness: it depends on the metal. In these tables uncoated steel sheet is the Manufacturers' Standard Gauge, galvanized sheet is listed thicker than uncoated steel of the same number, the stainless steel column is the same as the older US Standard gauge, and the aluminium column is the Brown & Sharpe (American wire) gauge. Order sheet by its thickness and give the gauge only as a name. The two publishers print stainless No. 9 as 0.1563 and 0.1562 in: it is 5/32 in (0.15625) rounded up by one and cut off by the other. For aluminium No. 14 and No. 26 one prints the Brown & Sharpe sizes (0.0641, 0.0159 in) and the other 0.063 and 0.017 in; both are shown. Zinc and copper sheet gauges are not included.",
  "wire": "Three different wire gauges share the same numbers. The American wire gauge (AWG, Brown & Sharpe), used mainly in North America for electrical and other nonferrous wire, is worked out here from its rule: 0.005 in x 92^((36 - n) / 39), with No. 36 = 0.005 in and No. 4/0 = 0.46 in. The British Standard Wire Gauge (SWG, BS 3737:1964, withdrawn) is a table. The third column is the series both publishers print for the wall thickness of steel tube and for strip; from No. 7 to 17 it has the same values as the Birmingham gauge, also called the Stubs iron wire gauge. Sizes larger than 0 are written 2/0 (00), 3/0 (000) and so on."
